From: NeoDarwin
Subject: [PATCH] vm: an empty shared region is not an error on SBSA

dyld calls shared_region_check_np twice in every exec to find the shared
cache a previous process mapped. NeoDarwin has no dyld shared cache, so the
region is always empty, vm_shared_region_start_address returns
KERN_INVALID_ADDRESS, and the default trace level (ERROR) printed

  shared_region: 0x... [846(rm)] check_np(0x16f45f8e0)
  vm_shared_region_start_address() returned 0x1

twice per exec on the console. On a slow serial console that costs time,
and the lines land in the middle of user output: //kernel:sbsa_rtadvd_test
lost expected lines that way under host load, with rtsold running
resolvconf(8) on every router advertisement. On macOS the answer is wrong
only until launchd maps the cache, so it is rare there.

Under GENERIC_ARM64_PLATFORM that one answer is traced at INFO
(vm.shared_region_trace_level=2 shows it); dyld's ENOMEM is unchanged, and
every other check_np failure (update_task, auth_remap, copyout) and every
mapping error stays at ERROR.

Rebase-risk: low
---
--- a/bsd/vm/vm_unix.c
+++ b/bsd/vm/vm_unix.c
@@ -1035,6 +1035,22 @@
 		} else {
 			/* retrieve address of its first mapping... */
 			kr = vm_shared_region_start_address(shared_region, &start_address);
+#if defined(GENERIC_ARM64_PLATFORM)
+			/*
+			 * NeoDarwin has no dyld shared cache, so the region is
+			 * always empty (KERN_INVALID_ADDRESS) and dyld asks twice
+			 * per exec: an expected answer, traced as INFO, not an
+			 * error on the console. Other failures stay errors.
+			 */
+			if (kr == KERN_INVALID_ADDRESS) {
+				SHARED_REGION_TRACE_INFO(("shared_region: %p [%d(%s)] "
+				    "check_np(0x%llx) no shared cache mapped\n",
+				    (void *)VM_KERNEL_ADDRPERM(current_thread()),
+				    proc_getpid(p), p->p_comm,
+				    (uint64_t)uap->start_address));
+				error = ENOMEM;
+			} else
+#endif /* GENERIC_ARM64_PLATFORM */
 			if (kr != KERN_SUCCESS) {
 				SHARED_REGION_TRACE_ERROR(("shared_region: %p [%d(%s)] "
 				    "check_np(0x%llx) "
